def is_convex_polygon(points):
    def cross(v1, v2):
        return v1[0] * v2[1] - v1[1] * v2[0]

    n = len(points)
    for i in range(n):
        p, q, r = points[i], points[(i + 1) % n], points[(i + 2) % n]
        v1 = (q[0] - p[0], q[1] - p[1])
        v2 = (r[0] - q[0], r[1] - q[1])
        if cross(v1, v2) > 0:
            sign = True
        elif cross(v1, v2) < 0:
            sign = False
        else:
            # Collinear points. A convex polygon needs every interior angle strictly less than 180 degrees.
            return False
        for j in range(i + 1, n):
            p, q, r = points[j], points[(j + 1) % n], points[(j + 2) % n]
            v1 = (q[0] - p[0], q[1] - p[1])
            v2 = (r[0] - q[0], r[1] - q[1])
            if (cross(v1, v2) > 0) != sign:
                return False
    return True
```

The central invariant of a convex polygon is that every interior angle measures strictly less than 180 degrees. The sign of the cross product of two adjacent edge vectors at each vertex encodes whether the turn at that vertex is left or right: a consistent sign across all vertices means all interior angles are obtuse or right, and the polygon is convex. The function computes the sign of a single vertex and then scans every other vertex, returning False whenever the sign flips.

