        d, e = cross(o, v, p), cross(o, v, w)
        if d * e < 0:
            return False
    return True
```

The decision is made with three signs of barycentric cross products. For each pair o and v of triangle vertices, the sign of (v - o) cross (p - o) tells whether p lies on the left or right of the edge from o to v, counting counterclockwise as the left. If p ever strays to the right of any such edge, the triangle does not contain it. A vertex of the triangle strays outside its own edge, so the sign comparison never succeeds, and reaching the return means all three edges face the same way: counterclockwise with the origin on the left and the test point sitting at the origin. That happens exactly when p is on an edge at a vertex, or inside the triangle.

